::: {.doc-subject}

Add a cycle graph (closed loop) to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L7846-L7970){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_cycle_graph(
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Cycle graph](../assets/graphs/add-cycle-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three cycle graphs side by side: a triangle for n=3, a square for n=4, and a hexagon for n=6. Each is a single closed loop in which every node has exactly two neighbors." width="100%"}

Creates n nodes in a circular pattern where the last connects back to first. Each node has
exactly degree `2`.

A cycle graph (often written C_n) is a path of `n` nodes plus one extra edge closing the
loop, so it has `n` nodes and `n` edges. Every node plays the same role, which means
centrality measures give all nodes the same score, and removing any single edge leaves a
connected path. Cycles are useful for:

- testing algorithms that detect cycles, bipartiteness (even `n` is bipartite, odd `n` is
  not), or girth
- modelling ring-shaped structures like ring networks, circular routes, or round-robin
  schedules
- serving as a symmetric baseline when comparing centrality or clustering results

Nodes are labelled exactly as in `add_path_graph()`: each with its node ID as a string
(`"1"` to `"n"` in an empty graph). In a directed
graph the edges run `1 -> 2 -> ... -> n -> 1`, forming a directed cycle. The cycle is added
as a new, separate component, the graph is modified in place, and the construction is
recorded as a single step in the step log (see `steps`).
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the cycle (minimum `3`, the triangle). The cycle will also have
    `n` edges.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional node type/category assigned to all new nodes (stored in the `type` column of
    the node DataFrame). Default is `None`, which leaves the type unset.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), label each node with its ID as described above (a label that is
    already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added). If `False`, the nodes are
    left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional relationship label assigned to all edges of the cycle, including the closing
    edge (stored in the `rel` column of the edge DataFrame). Default is `None`.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` is less than `3`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_path_graph`) 

:   Add a path graph.

[](`~add_circulant_graph`) 

:   Add a circulant graph.

[](`~is_bipartite`) 

:   Check whether the graph is bipartite.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A cycle graph is a path with one more edge, from the last node back to the first, so it
has as many edges as nodes and every node has two neighbors. In a directed graph the edges
all run the same way round, and the last one, here `4 -> 1`, closes the loop:

::: {#8860c7e2 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

ring = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_cycle_graph(n=4, rel="next")

ring.count_edges(), ring.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(4, ['1->2', '2->3', '3->4', '4->1'])
```
:::
:::


### Even and odd cycles

A cycle with an even number of nodes is bipartite: its nodes can be colored with two colors
so that no edge joins two nodes of the same color. An odd cycle needs a third color
somewhere. A coloring of the 6-cycle on the left and of the 7-cycle on the right:

::: {#e4bda8b0 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
even = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(n=6)
odd = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(n=7)

even.set_node_attrs("color", even.greedy_color())
odd.set_node_attrs("color", odd.greedy_color())

even.is_bipartite(), odd.is_bipartite()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


